Code caches' blocks come back after a VirtualProtect that left their code as it was (FEX 3bec2ac, on top of 0028)

With the DEP fix (0027) NFSU2 still ran 64 blocks of SPEED2.EXE in FEX's
NoJIT interpreter, every time. 59 of them were on 14 pages of its .text
that something (its hook installer, Ultimate ASI Loader and the
widescreen fix) makes executable again with VirtualProtect:

  [code-inval] executable protection set (alloc/protect): 0x5bf000-0x5c0000 dropped 33 blocks

FEX drops a page's blocks whenever it is made executable, since code may
have been written to it while it was not, and nothing registered the
code caches' blocks again.

- ImageTracker hashes every 64 bytes of an image's executable sections
  when it maps an image it has a code cache for (XXH3, 32 bits a chunk);
- InvalidationTracker::HandleMemoryProtectionNotification says whether it
  dropped code because a range was made executable, and
  BTCpuNotifyMemoryProtect then calls ImageTracker::RevalidateLoadedCaches;
- that registers again, through the new CodeCache::EnableLoadedBlocks,
  the cached blocks with a code page in the range whose code did not
  change: a block whose pages all hash as mapped comes back as it is; a
  block on a page that changed (a hook patched other code on it) is
  decoded again from the current bytes, and comes back when it decodes to
  the same code pages and none of its instruction bytes changed. A block
  whose bytes changed stays out and is compiled (or interpreted) from the
  new bytes;
- "[aot-cache] N blocks of IMAGE registered again after a protection
  change at guest A-B; K of its code pages there changed since the image
  was mapped" (first 64).

